    "https://www.vacancies.st-andrews.ac.uk/Vacancies/W/1189/0/476081/889/post-doctoral-research-fellow-ar3357": {
      "title": "Post-doctoral Research Fellow",
      "description": "Post-doctoral Research Fellow - AR3357 Register Login Back to search results Post-doctoral Research Fellow - AR3357 Applications are sought for a committed post-doctoral\nresearch fellow in evolutionary genomics to work on a 3-year project entitled: Testing\nthe genetic coupling hypothesis of speciation. The project, led by Prof Nathan Bailey\nat the University of St Andrews, tests one of the most prominent, but\nsurprisingly under-studied, theories explaining rapid divergence via sexual trait-preference\ncoevolution: the genetic coupling model of speciation. We will comprehensively\ntest this model using ultra-high resolution genetic mapping, comparative\ngenomics, and gene knockdown studies in the original group of organisms in\nwhich it was proposed: field crickets. The project involves\ncollaborators Prof Michael Ritchie at the University of St Andrews, Prof Thomas\nSchmitt at the University of Würzburg, and Prof Xiao Zhang at Tianjin Normal\nUniversity. The primary duties will be to: Perform fieldwork to collect multiple species of a well-studied southeast\n    Asian field cricket genus, Teleogryllus Further information and informal enquiries may be directed toSequence and annotate chromosomally contiguous reference genomes Phenotype reproductive barrier traits including acoustic and chemical\n    preferences Perform ultra-high resolution genetic association mapping to identify\n    reproductive barrier loci Analyze the evolutionary history of barrier loci using comparative\n    genomic approaches Functionally validate candidates using gene knockdowns Analyze data, write and submit manuscripts, and present at conferences The successful applicant will have (or be near\nto completion of) a PhD in evolutionary biology/evolutionary genomics, with\nexpertise in bioinformatic analysis of next-generation sequencing data and\nfunctional validation approaches. Experience with the following specific methods\nis desirable: genome assembly and annotation using long read, Hi-C, and\ntranscriptome data; behavioural analysis of sexual traits and preferences;\ncomparative genomics; RNAi, fieldwork. The post is available for 36 months starting 11th\nJanuary 2027 or as soon as possible thereafter. Information enquiries can be directed to Nathan Bailey: nwb3@st-andrews.ac.uk. Closing Date: 13 November 2026 Interview Date: 30 November 2026 Please Quote Ref: AR3357CM Job Description: AR3357CM FPs.pdf Biology (Centre for Biological Diversity) Salary: Grade 6, £38,784 - £46,049 per annum Start Date: 11 January 2027 or as soon as possible thereafter Hours: 36.25 hours per week, Full Time Duration: Fixed Term for 36 months Apply now Back to search results Post-doctoral Research Fellow - AR3357 School/Unit: School of Biology Posted: 23/10/2026 Closing date: 13/11/2026 Closing time: 23:59 (GMT) Apply now Save Share Login Register Email Address: Please enter a valid email address. Password: Sign in Forgot password? Your password has expired, please create a new password below. Your email address has not been verified. Please check your email or click here to resend. Verification email has been resent. Email Address: Please enter a valid email address. Reset password Cancel Email Address: New Password: At least 8 characters Uppercase Lowercase Symbols Numbers Confirm Password: Update Password Your login attempt was not successful. Please try again Please select Dr Miss Mr Mrs Ms Other Professor Reverend Reverend DR Sir Dr. des. Assistant Professor Mx Title * First name * Last name * Email address * Password * At least 8 characters Uppercase Lowercase Symbols Numbers Confirm password * I have read and understood the terms and conditions I have read and understood the data protection statement Create Account Cancel An email has been sent to your email address. Please confirm that your email address is active by clicking the link in the email. Powered by engage|ats Havas People a Havas Company This site is protected by reCAPTCHA. Contact Us - Application Query Terms and Conditions About Privacy Policy Cookies Accessibility FAQs"
    },
    "https://www.vacancies.st-andrews.ac.uk/Vacancies/W/1843/0/475819/889/lecturer-ac2923cm": {
      "title": "Lecturer",
      "description": "Lecturer - AC2923CM Register Login Back to search results Lecturer - AC2923CM The School of Computer Science is\nseeking to appoint a Lecturer in Cyber Security. You will be an emerging\nscholar with the potential to develop an internationally recognised research\nprofile and a strong commitment to delivering high-quality, research-led\nteaching in cyber security and related areas of Computer Science. We welcome applications from dynamic and\ncollegial candidates whose research will complement and extend the School’s\nexisting strengths. Relevant areas include, but are not limited to, systems and\nnetwork security, software security, applied cryptography, privacy-enhancing\ntechnologies, privacy in computer systems and computer communication, usable\nsecurity, the security of emerging technologies, and the human, organisational\nand societal dimensions of cyber security. The successful candidate will establish\nan ambitious programme of original research, pursue external funding and\nmeaningful impact, and contribute to excellent teaching and supervision across\nundergraduate and postgraduate programmes. Candidates should hold (or\nbe nearing completion of)\na PhD in cyber security, computer science or a closely related discipline .\nThey should demonstrate the potential to deliver excellent teaching, supervise\nstudent research, and contribute to curriculum development. An interest in\nknowledge exchange, and an understanding of the processes involved in securing\nfunding from research councils and other external sources are also essential. St Andrews offers more than just a place\nto work—it is a place to belong. We value collegiality, inclusion,\nand wellbeing, and we are committed to creating\na workplace\nwhere staff can thrive and perform at their best. In addition to the salary on offer for\nthis position, there are a wide range of benefits for staff working at the\nUniversity of St Andrews. The package for the Lecturer\nrole includes: Financial\n    contribution to relocation (subject to eligibility) Membership\n    of the USS Pension Scheme with a generous employer contribution of 14.5% A\n    hybrid working environment, including partial homeworking where appropriate and\n    a range of family friendly policies, supporting work-life balance Staff\n    discount scheme for local and national goods and services Free\n    staff parking, employee Carshare and Cycle to Work Schemes and subsidised local\n    bus travel Subsidised\n    sports membership, reduced tuition fees on degree programmes for staff, access\n    to training and development opportunities including LinkedIn Learning, access\n    to library facilities, salary sacrifice scheme 34\n    Days Annual Leave plus 5 Public Holidays At St\nAndrews, equality, diversity, and inclusion are central to who we are and form\na core pillar of our University Strategy. We are committed to creating a fair,\ninclusive and supportive workplace, reflected in our participation in national\ndiversity charters and awards, including Athena Swan, Carer Positive and the\nRace Equality Charter. Applications\nare particularly welcome from women who would be joining our multicultural\nSchool which includes staff and students from across the world. Women comprise over 25% of the BSc and 35% of the MSc cohorts, and account for 25% of our\nacademic staff. Applications are particularly welcome from people\nfrom the Black, Asian and Minority Ethnic (BAME) community, and other protected\ncharacteristics who are under-represented in academic posts at the University. The University encourages all\ninterested candidates to apply, and all applications received are assessed\nagainst the essential and desirable criteria listed in the further\nparticulars.  The successful candidate will have to demonstrate their\nright to work in the UK prior to commencing employment and where required, the University can support with visa\nsponsorship. Closing Date: 16 November 2026 Interview Date: 14 December 2026 Please Quote Ref: AC2923CM Job Description: AC2923CM FPs.pdf Computer Science Salary: Grade 7, £47,389 - £58,226 per annum Start Date: To Be Confirmed Duration: Standard, Full Time Apply now Back to search results Lecturer - AC2923CM School/Unit: School of Computer Science Posted: 17/10/2026 Closing date: 16/11/2026 Closing time: 23:59 (GMT) Apply now Save Share Login Register Email Address: Please enter a valid email address. Password: Sign in Forgot password? Your password has expired, please create a new password below. Your email address has not been verified. Please check your email or click here to resend. Verification email has been resent. Email Address: Please enter a valid email address. Reset password Cancel Email Address: New Password: At least 8 characters Uppercase Lowercase Symbols Numbers Confirm Password: Update Password Your login attempt was not successful. Please try again Please select Dr Miss Mr Mrs Ms Other Professor Reverend Reverend DR Sir Dr. des. Assistant Professor Mx Title * First name * Last name * Email address * Password * At least 8 characters Uppercase Lowercase Symbols Numbers Confirm password * I have read and understood the terms and conditions I have read and understood the data protection statement Create Account Cancel An email has been sent to your email address. Please confirm that your email address is active by clicking the link in the email. Powered by engage|ats Havas People a Havas Company This site is protected by reCAPTCHA. Contact Us - Application Query Terms and Conditions About Privacy Policy Cookies Accessibility FAQs"
